## 10. Existing 3D & scroll machinery you should reuse (not rebuild)

This is the most important context for all three planned features — the patterns already exist.

**`lib/webgl.ts`** — the capability gate. `hasWebGL()` actually *creates* a context (`webgl2` → `webgl` → `experimental-webgl`) and immediately releases it via `WEBGL_lose_context`. `isLowCapabilityDevice()` returns true on `pointer: coarse` **or** `max-width: 1023px` **or** `deviceMemory < 4` **or** `hardwareConcurrency < 4`. `shouldRenderWebGL(reducedMotion)` = `!reducedMotion && !isLowCapabilityDevice() && hasWebGL()`.

Note the sharp edge for your plans: **this gate refuses WebGL on every phone and every viewport under 1024px.** A mandatory full-screen 3D voice gate cannot use it as-is — you'd need a documented non-3D path for mobile, or a relaxed gate.

**`components/webgl-boundary.tsx`** — class error boundary; `getDerivedStateFromError` → renders `fallback ?? null`, `console.warn("[webgl] scene failed, falling back")`.

**`components/hero/scroll-frame-sequence.tsx` (207 lines)** — this is the existing scroll-driven sequence, and it is the closest thing to feature (b). Canvas 2D image-sequence scrubbing: preloads N frames as `ImageBitmap` (via `createImageBitmap`, falling back to `<img>`), scheduled behind `requestIdleCallback`. **Progress is sampled inside rAF, not a scroll listener** — deliberately, "so it stays in step with Lenis (which drives scrolling from its own loop)". Core math:

```tsx
const rect = node.getBoundingClientRect();
const travel = rect.height - window.innerHeight;
if (travel <= 0) return;
const progress = Math.min(1, Math.max(0, -rect.top / travel));
const index = Math.min(frames.length - 1, Math.round(progress * (frames.length - 1)));
```

Markup shape: outer `<div className="h-[250svh]">` → `<div className="sticky top-0 h-svh w-full overflow-hidden">` → poster `<img fetchPriority="high">` + `<canvas>` (fades in only once `hasPainted`) + `{children}`. Skips entirely on reduced motion, `connection.saveData`, or `effectiveType` matching `/(^|-)2g/`. DPR capped at `Math.min(devicePixelRatio, 1.5)`. Exposes `surface.dataset.frame` for tests.

**`components/hero/home-hero.tsx`** — `"use client"`, wraps `ScrollFrameSequence` with `frameCount={36}`, `framePath = i => \`/frames/hero/${String(i).padStart(3,"0")}.webp\``, `poster="/frames/hero/001.webp"`, `className="relative h-[250svh]"`. Children are the scrim, H1, CTAs (CSS `animate-[rise_…_var(--ease-entrance)_both]`), and a "Scroll" nudge. **`HeroCanvas`/`HeroScene` (R3F) exist but are not currently mounted by `HomeHero`** — the frame sequence replaced them. They remain as a working reference.

**`components/projects/master-plan.tsx` (81 lines)** — the canonical **two-gate** 3D mount, and the template for feature (c): `shouldRenderWebGL(reducedMotion)` → `IntersectionObserver({ rootMargin: "300px" })` → `requestIdleCallback` → `setAllowed(true)` → `<WebGLBoundary><MasterPlan3D/></WebGLBoundary>`. *"Two gates, not one. Idle keeps the decision off the hydration critical path; the intersection check keeps the Three.js chunk from downloading until the visitor is actually approaching the massing model."* Renders **nothing** when gated out.

**`components/projects/master-plan-3d.tsx` (217 lines)** — the working R3F reference. `<Canvas dpr={[1,1.5]} camera={{position:[7,6,9], fov:40}} gl={{antialias:true}}>`, `<color attach="background">`, ambient + two directional lights (`#f5f1e8` key, `#b99c6b` fill), `<OrbitControls enablePan={false} minPolarAngle={Math.PI/6} maxPolarAngle={Math.PI/2.2} minDistance={8} maxDistance={18} enableDamping dampingFactor={0.08}>`. Tower height = `Math.max(2, tower.floors * 0.28)`; materials and a `palette` of `THREE.Color`s are `useMemo`'d **once**, and `useFrame` only does `material.color.lerp(target, 0.12)` — the docblock calls out why: *"Allocating a THREE.Color inside the frame loop is the classic way to turn a smooth scene into a stuttering one."* Crucially it pairs the canvas with **keyboard-operable `<button>` tower selection**: *"OrbitControls alone is not accessible, so the same choice exists as buttons."*

**`components/hero/hero-scene.tsx` (120 lines)** — `Environment preset="dawn"`, `ACESFilmicToneMapping` at exposure 1.05, fog, a deterministic `BLOCKS` array, shared materials, pointer-parallax in `useFrame`.

**`components/projects/pannellum-tour.tsx`** — the existing "interior walkthrough": IO-gated, `await import("pannellum/build/pannellum.css")` + `.js` inside an effect (UMD, attaches to `window`), `autoRotate: -2`, `mouseZoom: "fullscreenonly"`, `setFailed` on throw. Serves `/panoramas/demo-interior.png`. **A scrollable 3D interior would most naturally replace this section** on the project page.

**Assets**: `public/frames/hero/` (36 webp stills), `public/panoramas/demo-interior.png`, `public/images/projects/*.webp`, `public/brochures/demo-brochure.pdf`. `scripts/generate-frames.mjs` + `scripts/extract-frames.md` document the frame pipeline. **No `.glb`/`.gltf`/`.hdr`/`.ktx2` anywhere.**

